m('NTP was also a notorious **DDoS amplifier**: the old `monlist` command returned up to 600 recent clients in reply to one tiny spoofed packet, an amplification of several hundred times. It has been disabled in current software.','Legacy ntpd\'s unauthenticated `monlist` response could amplify spoofed requests, documented as CVE-2013-5211. Exposure depends on implementation, version and access controls. A universal amplification ratio and present-day deployment prevalence are omitted because this review did not establish reliable measurements.')
m('For sub-microsecond accuracy (trading, telecoms, 5G), data centres use **PTP** (IEEE 1588), with hardware timestamping in the NIC and switches that correct for their own queueing delay.','**PTP** (IEEE 1588) can achieve sub-microsecond synchronisation in appropriately engineered networks. Hardware timestamping and PTP-aware transparent clocks reduce timestamp and switch-residence errors; the protocol name alone does not guarantee a particular accuracy.')
