m('On first connection there\'s nothing to check against, hence the "authenticity of host can\'t be established" prompt: **trust on first use** (TOFU).','Without a preconfigured trusted host key or host-certificate authority, a first connection can prompt for **trust on first use** (TOFU). Independently checking the fingerprint avoids blindly trusting that first peer.')
m('After `NEWKEYS`, every packet uses the **binary packet protocol**:','After the version strings, SSH already uses the **binary packet protocol**; initial key-exchange packets are unencrypted. `NEWKEYS` activates the negotiated protection. The base packet structure is:')
m('The padding makes the total a multiple of the cipher block size (at least 8) and hides exact lengths a little.','In the base RFC 4253 format, packet length including its four-byte length field but excluding the MAC is aligned to the cipher block size or 8 bytes, whichever is larger. Negotiated AEAD/Encrypt-then-MAC formats alter some framing and authentication details. Padding provides limited length hiding.')
m('Each side keeps an implicit **sequence number** per direction, included in the MAC, so reordered or dropped packets are detected.','Each direction has an implicit **sequence number**, used by its negotiated integrity construction. This normally detects packet insertion/deletion; the handshake interaction below exposed an exception for affected modes.')
m('An attacker could inject a message during the unencrypted phase and delete one afterwards,','With affected encryption modes (notably ChaCha20-Poly1305 or CBC with Encrypt-then-MAC), an on-path attacker could inject a message during the unencrypted phase and delete one afterwards,')
m('Anyone with root on that host can use your agent while you\'re connected.','A privileged attacker on that host can request signatures through an unconstrained forwarded agent while it remains accessible. Confirmation, destination and hardware constraints can limit use; forwarding does not normally reveal the private key itself.')
m('Port-forwarding a TCP connection through SSH also means TCP inside TCP: loss on the outer connection stalls every channel.','Ordinary SSH port forwarding relays application bytes between separate TCP connections; it does not encapsulate complete inner TCP packets. Loss on the shared SSH TCP connection can nevertheless stall delivery for every multiplexed channel.')
